// The masonry arch of the FBF paper's examples (github.com/matthcsong/fbf-sca-2026,
// paper_examples/masonry-arch/run.py, meshes/arch/num_stones=25), shared by the PhysX and MuJoCo
// runners: 25 wedge-shaped stones, each the convex hull of 8 vertices, forming a pointed arch
// about 62 m high and 73 m across, 10 m deep, stone density 2000 and friction 0.8. The end stones
// (abutments) are fixed. Neighbouring faces are parallel but built 120-141 mm apart, so the free
// stones fall into place and the arch settles under its own weight.
//
// y is up, as in the meshes. Vertices are in metres, in the stone's frame: relative to the mean
// of its vertices, which is its position (as the paper's runner places them).
